1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,749
The same fix is on main as cf6a5b3,
and on the release branch as e460212.

2
00:00:09,789 --> 00:00:13,807
Your CTO asks: is that one
fix, or two different things?

3
00:00:14,147 --> 00:00:19,084
If a commit is a diff, a list of changed
lines, they're the same thing and should

4
00:00:19,124 --> 00:00:23,876
have the same ID. They don't. If a commit
is a snapshot, a full picture of the

5
00:00:23,916 --> 00:00:28,915
project, they're different things. But they
have the same message, the same author

6
00:00:28,955 --> 00:00:32,110
date, and they change the
same line. So which is it?

7
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:33,093
Say it out loud.

8
00:00:37,933 --> 00:00:42,106
Both halves are true, and you need to
know which half each command uses.

9
00:00:42,146 --> 00:00:46,787
In this video you put the model together,
and then you use it to take apart four

10
00:00:46,827 --> 00:00:49,946
wrong models that most
confusion about Git comes from.

11
00:00:49,986 --> 00:00:52,521
The CTO gets a one-line
answer near the end.

12
00:00:54,361 --> 00:00:57,993
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:58,033 --> 00:01:02,026
This is the last video of the object
model, and it has three parts.

14
00:01:02,366 --> 00:01:06,887
First, the three trees: a preview of the
next block of the course, in which a file

15
00:01:06,927 --> 00:01:11,559
exists in three states at the same time.
Second, the two views of a commit: snapshot

16
00:01:11,599 --> 00:01:15,897
and change. Third, the map of what lives
where: your laptop, the server, and a

17
00:01:15,937 --> 00:01:20,347
colleague's clone, with one rule for what
can cross between repositories at all.

18
00:01:20,687 --> 00:01:25,578
Along the way we refute four wrong models
with a transcript each: Git stores diffs,

19
00:01:25,618 --> 00:01:29,619
a branch is a copy, a commit belongs
to a branch, and GitHub is Git.

20
00:01:29,659 --> 00:01:34,431
If you hold one of them right now, you're
in very large company, and the textbook

21
00:01:34,471 --> 00:01:35,382
has the numbers.

22
00:01:37,222 --> 00:01:38,800
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:40,552
After this video you can:

24
00:01:40,892 --> 00:01:44,632
Name the three trees, and say
which command compares which pair.

25
00:01:44,972 --> 00:01:48,924
Explain how a commit can be read as
a snapshot and as a change without

26
00:01:48,964 --> 00:01:49,723
contradiction.

27
00:01:50,063 --> 00:01:53,476
Refute four common wrong models
of Git with a transcript each.

28
00:01:53,816 --> 00:01:58,586
Say which parts of a repository travel to
the server on a push, and which never do.

29
00:02:00,426 --> 00:02:05,296
The three trees. In one sentence: a file
can exist in three states at the same time,

30
00:02:05,336 --> 00:02:10,147
in the commit that HEAD names, in the index
and in the working tree, and git status

31
00:02:10,187 --> 00:02:12,310
reports the differences between them.

32
00:02:12,650 --> 00:02:17,190
Now precisely. "Tree" here means a
complete set of files, not a tree object.

33
00:02:17,230 --> 00:02:22,192
The commit that HEAD names is the last
snapshot, and HEAD is the name for where you

34
00:02:22,232 --> 00:02:27,193
are now. The index is the proposed next
snapshot: every tracked path with the ID of

35
00:02:27,233 --> 00:02:31,171
a blob, one file's content. The
working tree is the files on disk.

36
00:02:31,211 --> 00:02:36,052
git add copies content from the working
tree into the index, and git commit turns

37
00:02:36,092 --> 00:02:38,522
the index into tree objects and a commit.

38
00:02:38,862 --> 00:02:43,734
Two commands compare neighbouring pairs.
git diff --cached compares HEAD with the

39
00:02:43,774 --> 00:02:47,191
index. git diff compares the
index with the working tree.

40
00:02:47,231 --> 00:02:51,011
Neither shows the whole distance
from HEAD to the working tree.

41
00:02:51,051 --> 00:02:52,163
git diff HEAD does.

42
00:02:52,503 --> 00:02:57,164
Two views of a commit. "A commit is a diff"
is wrong as a statement about storage,

43
00:02:57,204 --> 00:03:01,921
and right as a way to read history. You
need both views, and you need to know which

44
00:03:01,961 --> 00:03:03,010
one a command uses.

45
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:06,783
The snapshot view: the tree
that the commit records.

46
00:03:06,823 --> 00:03:12,192
It is used by checkout, by builds, by git
ls-tree, by git diff A B, and by merge,

47
00:03:12,232 --> 00:03:14,262
which works on three snapshots.

48
00:03:14,602 --> 00:03:20,162
The change view: the difference between the
commit's tree and its parent's, computed

49
00:03:20,202 --> 00:03:25,629
on demand. It is used by git show, by git
log -p, by cherry-pick, by rebase and by

50
00:03:25,669 --> 00:03:26,095
revert.

51
00:03:26,435 --> 00:03:31,043
Take cherry-pick as the test case, with
the two branches from the hook on screen.

52
00:03:31,083 --> 00:03:34,428
Cherry-pick copies one commit's
change onto another branch.

53
00:03:34,468 --> 00:03:38,960
The manual describes it in the change
view: it applies the change that a commit

54
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,665
introduces and records a new commit. The
implementation is a three-way merge whose

55
00:03:43,705 --> 00:03:47,796
base is the parent of the picked
commit, so it works on three snapshots.

56
00:03:48,136 --> 00:03:52,859
Rebase repeats this for a series of commits,
and revert applies the inverse change.

57
00:03:52,899 --> 00:03:57,680
The result is always a new commit with a
new ID. git cherry-pick carries the label 🟡

58
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:02,386
CAUTION: it adds one commit to the current
branch, which moves it, and updates the

59
00:04:02,426 --> 00:04:03,935
index and the working tree.

60
00:04:04,275 --> 00:04:07,582
The wrong models. The textbook's
table has eleven rows.

61
00:04:07,622 --> 00:04:11,171
Here are the four this video
proves, each with its reality.

62
00:04:11,511 --> 00:04:16,924
Wrong: a commit stores a diff. Reality: a
commit records a full snapshot, the ID of

63
00:04:16,964 --> 00:04:21,392
the top-level tree, with its parents,
author, committer and message.

64
00:04:21,432 --> 00:04:25,268
Diffs are computed on demand.
You saw the proof in video 7.

65
00:04:25,608 --> 00:04:29,397
Wrong: a branch is a copy of
the code, with a parent branch.

66
00:04:29,437 --> 00:04:34,695
Reality: a branch is a ref that names one
commit, and Git has no notion of a parent

67
00:04:34,735 --> 00:04:39,290
branch. You saw the proof in video 9:
creating a branch added no object.

68
00:04:39,630 --> 00:04:44,751
Wrong: a commit belongs to a branch. Reality:
"the commits on a branch" is the set

69
00:04:44,791 --> 00:04:49,848
reachable from the commit the branch names.
One commit can be reachable from many

70
00:04:49,888 --> 00:04:52,554
refs or from none. You
saw both in video 9.

71
00:04:52,894 --> 00:04:57,244
Wrong: GitHub is Git, and in particular,
a pull request is a Git feature.

72
00:04:57,284 --> 00:05:02,296
Reality: it's a GitHub object. Today's last
demo shows what a clone carries and what

73
00:05:02,336 --> 00:05:02,958
it doesn't.

74
00:05:03,298 --> 00:05:08,150
The textbook adds a number, so that you
don't think these are beginner errors.

75
00:05:08,190 --> 00:05:13,418
In a poll of 2,466 mostly professional
developers, 50 percent think of a commit as a

76
00:05:13,458 --> 00:05:18,561
diff and 42 percent as a snapshot. The
textbook notes that this is a self-selected

77
00:05:18,601 --> 00:05:23,704
sample. Each group holds half of the model.
And in the same polls, only 10 percent

78
00:05:23,744 --> 00:05:27,592
of respondents were fully confident
that they understood HEAD.

79
00:05:27,932 --> 00:05:32,801
What travels. One rule: a push or a fetch
transfers objects and updates refs.

80
00:05:32,841 --> 00:05:36,244
Watch: a teammate pushes,
and a commit and a ref move.

81
00:05:36,284 --> 00:05:41,280
Then you fetch, and a commit and a ref
move. Nothing else in the dot git folder

82
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:46,444
leaves your machine. Not the index, not
the reflogs, not the config file, not the

83
00:05:46,484 --> 00:05:47,273
working tree.

84
00:05:49,113 --> 00:05:51,588
Hold three pictures, one for each part.

85
00:05:51,928 --> 00:05:55,896
Three boxes in a row: HEAD, index, working
tree. Two comparisons between neighbours.

86
00:05:55,936 --> 00:05:58,186
git status is those two
comparisons, summarized.

87
00:05:58,526 --> 00:06:02,863
One commit, two readings. As a
photograph, it's what a build contains.

88
00:06:02,903 --> 00:06:07,239
As a difference from its parent, it's
what a cherry-pick carries over.

89
00:06:07,279 --> 00:06:10,553
The photograph is stored.
The difference is computed.

90
00:06:10,893 --> 00:06:14,985
Two repositories and a wire. Only
objects and refs cross the wire.

91
00:06:15,325 --> 00:06:19,958
Where does this break? The third picture
shows only the Git layer of the server.

92
00:06:19,998 --> 00:06:24,864
GitHub has two more layers that no wire to
your clone carries at all: the platform's

93
00:06:24,904 --> 00:06:29,770
database, and GitHub Actions. And the first
picture is a preview: the index has more

94
00:06:29,810 --> 00:06:33,742
in it than one blob per path, and
the next block of videos opens it.

95
00:06:35,582 --> 00:06:39,964
Three boxes, one file, three versions:
one line, two lines, three lines.

96
00:06:40,004 --> 00:06:44,939
Under the boxes, the two comparisons, each
with the heading that git status gives

97
00:06:44,979 --> 00:06:49,913
it. A commit made now would record the
middle version: the index, not the file on

98
00:06:49,953 --> 00:06:53,844
disk. That's the mechanism behind
the worked example of video 6.

99
00:07:11,284 --> 00:07:15,880
One file is committed, then changed
and staged, then changed again.

100
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:20,862
Predict how many versions of rules.txt
exist at once, and where each is.

101
00:07:20,902 --> 00:07:22,731
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

102
00:07:27,571 --> 00:07:31,031
git show HEAD:rules.txt
prints the committed version.

103
00:07:31,071 --> 00:07:36,381
git show :rules.txt, with a colon and no
commit, prints the version in the index.

104
00:07:36,421 --> 00:07:40,212
cat prints the file on disk.
Three versions exist at once.

105
00:07:40,552 --> 00:07:43,495
The file is listed twice,
once for each comparison.

106
00:07:43,535 --> 00:07:48,349
That's the double listing you met in video
8, and now you know where it comes from.

107
00:07:48,689 --> 00:07:54,047
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: which
added line will git diff --cached show,

108
00:07:54,087 --> 00:07:57,884
and which will plain git diff
show? Write your answer down.

109
00:08:02,724 --> 00:08:10,314
git diff --cached: HEAD against the index,
one added line, the one about accents.

110
00:08:10,354 --> 00:08:18,132
git diff: index against working tree, another
added line, the one about whitespace.

111
00:08:18,172 --> 00:08:25,950
Now read the index lines of the two diffs:
bfb2990..298e6b4, then 298e6b4..969b4fa.

112
00:08:25,990 --> 00:08:30,753
The blob in the middle is
the same. It's the index.

113
00:08:31,093 --> 00:08:34,224
main is two commits ahead of release/1.0.

114
00:08:34,564 --> 00:08:38,808
The last commit on main, cf6a5b3,
changes one line in client.yaml.

115
00:08:38,848 --> 00:08:43,871
The commit before it, "Run four workers",
is not wanted on the release branch.

116
00:08:43,911 --> 00:08:48,610
git cherry-pick 🟡 CAUTION copies the
last commit onto the release branch.

117
00:08:48,650 --> 00:08:54,062
Quick quiz, two options. Option one: the
new commit has the same ID as the original.

118
00:08:54,102 --> 00:08:56,983
Option two: it has a new
ID. Say it out loud.

119
00:09:01,823 --> 00:09:05,695
Option two. A new commit,
e460212, with the same message.

120
00:09:05,735 --> 00:09:11,461
The Date: line is the author date, which a
cherry-pick takes over from the original.

121
00:09:11,801 --> 00:09:16,818
Now the question that separates the
two views. Predict: is the snapshot of

122
00:09:16,858 --> 00:09:19,962
release/1.0 now equal to
the snapshot of main?

123
00:09:24,802 --> 00:09:29,410
The snapshots differ. Both contain
the new client.yaml, blob efc3dd6.

124
00:09:29,450 --> 00:09:34,867
But server.yaml is d333cab on main and
c44fe52 on the release branch, which never

125
00:09:34,907 --> 00:09:40,256
received the commit "Run four workers".
Had cherry-pick copied the snapshot, the

126
00:09:40,296 --> 00:09:45,443
release would now run four workers.
Nobody wants to discover that on a Friday

127
00:09:45,483 --> 00:09:45,982
evening.

128
00:09:46,322 --> 00:09:51,550
And the change is the same. git patch-id
reduces a diff to an ID that ignores line

129
00:09:51,590 --> 00:09:56,561
numbers and whitespace, and both commits
give the same first column, beginning

130
00:09:56,601 --> 00:09:57,075
22602bf.

131
00:09:57,415 --> 00:10:02,284
Same change, different snapshot,
different parent, different commit ID.

132
00:10:02,324 --> 00:10:05,050
That's the answer to the CTO's question.

133
00:10:05,390 --> 00:10:09,600
A bare repository on disk plays
the Git layer of the server.

134
00:10:09,640 --> 00:10:15,196
Your clone has one commit, a second branch
wip/unicode, a staged change, and an

135
00:10:15,236 --> 00:10:19,092
alias st, a shortcut for a
command, in its .git/config.

136
00:10:19,132 --> 00:10:20,651
git push is 🟡 CAUTION.

137
00:10:20,991 --> 00:10:26,186
Four objects exist locally: the commit,
its tree, the committed blob, and the blob

138
00:10:26,226 --> 00:10:31,357
of the staged change, 298e6b4. Predict
what the server has: how many objects, how

139
00:10:31,397 --> 00:10:33,081
many refs, and which files.

140
00:10:37,921 --> 00:10:44,006
The server received three objects and one
ref. The staged blob is absent, because no

141
00:10:44,046 --> 00:10:49,766
pushed commit refers to it. The branch
wip/unicode is absent, because it wasn't

142
00:10:49,806 --> 00:10:55,599
pushed. There is no index, no logs/
directory and no working tree, so git status

143
00:10:55,639 --> 00:10:56,328
can't run.

144
00:10:56,668 --> 00:11:01,550
A colleague's clone. git clone is 🟢
SAFE. It has the server's branch as the

145
00:11:01,590 --> 00:11:06,932
remote-tracking branch refs/remotes/origin/main,
and Git created a local main from

146
00:11:06,972 --> 00:11:11,066
it. The reflog begins with the
clone. And the alias is unknown.

147
00:11:11,106 --> 00:11:14,348
Your reflogs and your
.git/config stayed with you.

148
00:11:16,188 --> 00:11:17,836
Five mistakes to watch for.

149
00:11:18,176 --> 00:11:21,422
Expecting a cherry-picked commit
to have the original's ID.

150
00:11:21,462 --> 00:11:26,102
Root cause: the commit ID is computed from
the tree and the parent as well; the same

151
00:11:26,142 --> 00:11:29,667
change on another parent is another
snapshot and another commit.

152
00:11:30,007 --> 00:11:34,967
Reading only git diff and missing what is
staged. Root cause: it compares the index

153
00:11:35,007 --> 00:11:39,968
with the working tree; git diff --cached
compares HEAD with the index, and git diff

154
00:11:40,008 --> 00:11:41,835
HEAD covers the whole distance.

155
00:11:42,175 --> 00:11:46,861
Counting on the reflog to recover something
on the server or in another clone.

156
00:11:46,901 --> 00:11:51,709
Root cause: reflogs are local and are not
shared with remotes; bare repositories

157
00:11:51,749 --> 00:11:52,981
keep none by default.

158
00:11:53,321 --> 00:11:58,424
Believing that a staged secret never left
a trace because it was never committed.

159
00:11:58,464 --> 00:12:03,566
Root cause: git add stores content at once;
the blob is an object in .git/objects

160
00:12:03,606 --> 00:12:05,852
until garbage collection removes it.

161
00:12:06,192 --> 00:12:10,624
Treating origin/main as the branch on the
server. Root cause: a remote-tracking

162
00:12:10,664 --> 00:12:15,097
branch records the last-known state of
the remote branch, as of the last fetch.

163
00:12:16,937 --> 00:12:21,852
Now, out of the lab. An inference team
maintains a release branch that must not

164
00:12:21,892 --> 00:12:26,994
receive a capacity change made on main. A
fix to the client's retry count lands on

165
00:12:27,034 --> 00:12:30,945
main after that capacity change.
The team cherry-picks the fix.

166
00:12:30,985 --> 00:12:35,711
The change view tells them what the
cherry-pick will carry over: one line in

167
00:12:35,751 --> 00:12:36,464
client.yaml.

168
00:12:36,804 --> 00:12:41,682
The snapshot view tells them what the
release build will contain afterwards: the

169
00:12:41,722 --> 00:12:46,663
retry fix, and still two workers. An
engineer who holds only the diff model can't

170
00:12:46,703 --> 00:12:51,520
explain why the two commits have different
IDs. One who holds only the snapshot

171
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:55,332
model fears that the release now
contains everything main has.

172
00:12:55,672 --> 00:13:00,061
And the sentence from the textbook for
incident channels: "It is on GitHub"

173
00:13:00,101 --> 00:13:04,727
translates to: which objects were pushed,
and which ref on the server points at

174
00:13:04,767 --> 00:13:09,393
them? "GitHub shows X" raises the question
whether X is Git data that any clone

175
00:13:09,433 --> 00:13:11,342
would show, or a platform record.

176
00:13:13,182 --> 00:13:19,861
Your turn. Do Exercise 1.1, Level 1,
"one commit, three object types", in

177
00:13:19,901 --> 00:13:22,898
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

178
00:13:23,238 --> 00:13:28,068
Before you open any object, write down how
many objects of each type the one commit

179
00:13:28,108 --> 00:13:31,411
will have created, and which
of them hold the file names.

180
00:13:33,251 --> 00:13:38,519
Q21: "Which parts of .git reach the
server when you push, and which never do?

181
00:13:38,559 --> 00:13:41,690
What does GitHub hold
that no clone contains?"

182
00:13:44,030 --> 00:13:48,555
Answer out loud. A strong answer states
the rule for what crosses between

183
00:13:48,595 --> 00:13:53,684
repositories in one sentence, then goes
through .git entry by entry and sorts each

184
00:13:53,724 --> 00:13:58,812
one. For the second half it names the layers
on the GitHub side and gives examples

185
00:13:58,852 --> 00:14:03,566
from each, and it mentions the refs that
GitHub itself writes on the server.

186
00:14:05,406 --> 00:14:10,259
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: a file can be in three states at

187
00:14:10,299 --> 00:14:13,990
once, in HEAD's commit, in the
index and in the working tree.

188
00:14:14,030 --> 00:14:18,026
git diff --cached compares the
first pair and git diff the second.

189
00:14:18,366 --> 00:14:23,042
A commit is stored as a snapshot and can
be read as a change against its parent.

190
00:14:23,082 --> 00:14:27,639
Checkout, builds and merge use the
snapshot, and show, cherry-pick, rebase and

191
00:14:27,679 --> 00:14:32,473
revert use the change. A cherry-pick makes
a new commit with the same change and a

192
00:14:32,513 --> 00:14:33,592
different snapshot.

193
00:14:33,932 --> 00:14:38,909
A push or a fetch transfers objects and
updates refs, and nothing else in .git

194
00:14:38,949 --> 00:14:44,248
leaves my machine. Pull requests, reviews
and workflow runs are GitHub records that

195
00:14:44,288 --> 00:14:45,406
no clone contains.

196
00:14:47,246 --> 00:14:53,502
Read sections 2.9 to 2.15 of Chapter
2, and do the Practice section 2.17.

197
00:14:53,842 --> 00:14:59,515
Challenge: Exercise 1.5, Level
2, "add twice, commit once", in

198
00:14:59,555 --> 00:15:02,556
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

199
00:15:02,896 --> 00:15:07,925
That's the object model, complete. You can
now read a commit from two sides, and say

200
00:15:07,965 --> 00:15:12,693
where every part of a repository lives.
Do the practice section before the next

201
00:15:12,733 --> 00:15:17,762
video. Next time: the working tree, three
categories of file, and the anatomy of git

202
00:15:17,802 --> 00:15:21,383
status. Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

203
00:15:21,423 --> 00:15:22,831
See you in the next one.
